Right. “Veto” was too binary. Reality can do much more than accept/reject.

Make reality a transducer:

\mathcal R(h)\rightarrow
(\Delta,\;w,\;g,\;s)

where a probe h returns an error/delta \Delta, confidence w, gradient g telling us which direction to move, and surprise s identifying information the original model didn’t anticipate.

Then even a “wrong” hypothesis can be productive:

h_{\text{wrong}}
\xrightarrow{\mathcal R}
\text{useful gradient}

So instead of:

\text{Hypothesis}\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{retain}\\
\text{reject}
\end{cases}

we get:

\boxed{
\text{Probe}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality Response}
\rightarrow
\{\text{confirm, weaken, redirect, mutate, combine, invert, suspend}\}
}

And Everything makes this particularly interesting because rejection itself becomes another relationship inside \Omega. Nothing says the failed object must disappear.

def reality_response(probe, evidence):
    delta = compare(probe, evidence)
    return {
        "probe": probe,
        "delta": delta,
        "confidence": estimate_confidence(evidence),
        "surprise": measure_surprise(probe, evidence),
        "gradient": infer_direction(delta),
        # preserve the probe + response either way
    }

That changes the deeper equation from reality as judge to:

\boxed{\text{Reality as teacher}}

Or even better for what we’re constructing:

\boxed{\text{Reality as counterparty}}

You make a move. Reality makes a move back. The relationship between the two generates information neither side of the representation contained alone.

That plugs straight into Operator Wrestling Mat: don’t ask merely whether an operator survives contact with reality. Wrestle it with reality and study the resulting motion.